1
00:00:02,470 --> 00:00:13,760
 Then we code together and then after lunch you have some labs where you can work on your exercises on your own pace there.

2
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:39,220
 So today, hopefully after today, you will be able to explain what NoSQL is and what MongoDB are and how they differ from SQL.

3
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:49,440
 No words like documents, collections, fields, ID and stuff like that.

4
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:58,370
 We look at your data using only Python with print and stuff.

5
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:06,150
 Connect to MongoDB from Python with a library called PyMongo.

6
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,920
 We insert and find documents.

7
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:14,490
 And don't worry if some of these words are new right now.

8
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:22,250
 We'll go through them each one by one, here in Therial at least.

9
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:26,130
 Then you will see it in action as usual.

10
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:45,280
 So for almost one week at least, we worked with SQL and data lives in tables.

11
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:54,880
 Every row had exactly the same columns and we used join to put pieces together.

12
00:01:56,910 --> 00:02:02,370
 This week, we will look at another family of databases called NoSQL.

13
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:07,780
 The name means actually not only SQL.

14
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:11,510
 So it's not that SQL is bad.

15
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:14,520
 NoSQL is just a different tool.

16
00:02:15,370 --> 00:02:17,530
 Some jobs are easier with it.

17
00:02:17,530 --> 00:02:32,430
 If you think back for a second, in SQL, before we could save a single customer, we had to write create table, if you remember that.

18
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:35,240
 And then decide every column in advance.

19
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:39,880
 So we need to know the first name, the last name, email, city and so on.

20
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:45,300
 And every customer had to fit into exactly those columns.

21
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:51,760
 So if a customer had two phone numbers, we needed a new table.

22
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:56,990
 And that's a very safe way to actually work.

23
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:02,590
 And this is why banks and booking systems love SQL.

24
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:08,200
 But it's also, if you ask me, it's also a bit stiff.

25
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:16,470
 And this week, hopefully, you will see a database where you don't have to decide everything in advance.

26
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:23,130
 Has anyone heard the word NoSQL before?

27
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:27,280
 Before you heard me saying it?

28
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:29,030
 You can raise your hands.

29
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:35,470
 Okay, okay.

30
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,650
 Almost half of you, at least.

31
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:40,900
 All right.

32
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,750
 Thank you.

33
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:43,950
 You can bring down your hands.

34
00:03:44,130 --> 00:03:54,230
 Hopefully, in the future, many of you will meet this at your future job.

35
00:03:55,010 --> 00:04:00,370
 Because a lot of modern apps use both SQL and NoSQL side by side.

36
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,650
 So this isn't instead of SQL.

37
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:05,190
 You shouldn't think of it like that.

38
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:08,310
 It's just one more tool in your toolbox.

39
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:15,510
 That's a question.

40
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:19,580
 Why would anyone use something other than SQL?

41
00:04:21,110 --> 00:04:23,220
 And here are actually four common reasons.

42
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:29,330
 Sometimes the data changes shape all the time.

43
00:04:29,330 --> 00:04:36,720
 For example, a new product type with new properties every week.

44
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,440
 In SQL, you need to change the tables each time.

45
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:50,510
 And sometimes the program thinks in objects like Python dictionaries.

46
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,630
 And we can turn them into tables.

47
00:04:56,590 --> 00:04:59,550
 Yeah, that would be pretty hard work.

48
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:09,150
 NoSQL databases are built to basically spread over many servers.

49
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:14,230
 Let's take each row with an example.

50
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:20,870
 So row one, imagine our web shop starts selling phones, for example.

51
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:31,210
 Phones have screen sizes, storage, color, or for example, shoes also have sizes, or t-shirt, or anything.

52
00:05:31,210 --> 00:05:39,500
 In SQL, you would need an extra table, extra tables, or lots of empty columns, actually.

53
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:46,430
 In NoSQL, each product just has basically the fields it needs.

54
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:57,200
 So row two, in Python, we would already think in dictionaries and lists, when we say row two.

55
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:06,680
 With SQL, we translate that, translate basically a dictionary into rows and back again every time.

56
00:06:07,410 --> 00:06:12,800
 With MongoDB, we just have, we just save that basically to dictionaries.

57
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:39,410
 If you remember the recipe with three joins, which we did, in NoSQL, we can keep the whole recipe together.

58
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:42,960
 So we don't have to use joins in the same way.

59
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:52,520
 I mean, in really big companies, have sometimes so much data that it doesn't fit in one computer.

60
00:06:53,210 --> 00:06:58,050
 So NoSQL databases were built from the start to spread the data over many servers.

61
00:07:00,890 --> 00:07:03,250
 Of course, you won't need to do that this week.

62
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:08,050
 But this is one of the main reasons NoSQL exists.

63
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:11,320
 MongoDB.

64
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:14,680
 The NoSQL database we'll use is MongoDB.

65
00:07:15,310 --> 00:07:16,640
 I'm going to take some coffee.

66
00:07:17,330 --> 00:07:17,550
 Hold on.

67
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:25,420
 And the reason is basically, yeah, it's the most popular one.

68
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:29,100
 And it's used by many big companies.

69
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:35,130
 MongoDB is a document database.

70
00:07:35,270 --> 00:07:39,670
 We call it, which means it stores data as documents.

71
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:46,800
 There is actually a community server, which is free and runs on your own computer here.

72
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,410
 Just like our SQLite file did.

73
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:57,120
 We will talk to it from Python with a library called PyMongo.

74
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,320
 So there are several kinds of NoSQL databases, but we will focus on one.

75
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:06,610
 And it's going to be MongoDB.

76
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:13,880
 The name actually comes from the word, can't remember.

77
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:19,640
 It was from a word called humongoose or something like that.

78
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:20,300
 I can't remember.

79
00:08:20,850 --> 00:08:26,600
 Which means basically huge because it was built to handle a lot of data.

80
00:08:30,420 --> 00:08:36,200
 And it's the most widely used NoSQL database, for example, in job ads.

81
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:38,980
 So this is a good one to know.

82
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:58,720
 MongoDB is, I mean, each piece of data, for example, one product is stored as one document.

83
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,710
 Because remember, MongoDB is a document database.

84
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:12,130
 Because you can install this on your own computer for free, which you hopefully will do today.

85
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:18,960
 A document.

86
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:20,020
 What is a document?

87
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:23,350
 Well, this is a document that you see in front of you.

88
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,260
 And it looks pretty familiar.

89
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,430
 It looks exactly like a Python dictionary.

90
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:30,690
 It's made of pairs.

91
00:09:32,230 --> 00:09:33,700
 Field name and a value.

92
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:36,190
 The name is hoodie black here.

93
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:39,500
 Price is $599.

94
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:43,750
 But look at the last two lines.

95
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:50,910
 Tags is a list inside this document.

96
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:55,900
 And details is a small dictionary inside the dictionary.

97
00:09:58,370 --> 00:10:06,450
 So in SQL, a list like tags would need its own table.

98
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,920
 But here it just lives inside the product.

99
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:20,290
 So if you read this document line by line here together.

100
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:24,930
 Underscore ID is one.

101
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:27,780
 That's the product ID.

102
00:10:29,470 --> 00:10:31,540
 The name is hoodie black.

103
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:35,020
 And then we have category here.

104
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:38,180
 Which is, yeah, well, we say it's clothing.

105
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:40,730
 And the price is $599.

106
00:10:41,030 --> 00:10:42,850
 A number without quotes.

107
00:10:42,850 --> 00:10:53,410
 And then we have tags, square brackets.

108
00:10:56,100 --> 00:11:00,170
 So it's a list with three, with basically three words in it.

109
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:05,710
 And then we have details here with curly brackets.

110
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:09,110
 So it's a small dictionary inside a big one.

111
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:13,990
 With basically color and material and stuff like that.

112
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:19,800
 So if I ask a question here to you guys.

113
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:27,940
 How many tables would we need in SQL to store this one hoodie with tags?

114
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:32,860
 Raise your hand.

115
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:42,840
 And I would say at least two.

116
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:48,880
 Products and products tags table.

117
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:50,480
 Maybe more.

118
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:54,430
 But here it's all in one place.

119
00:11:55,550 --> 00:11:57,910
 The format you see is called JSON.

120
00:11:58,820 --> 00:12:02,580
 Which stands for JavaScript Object Notation.

121
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:07,570
 It's the most common way to send data on the internet, actually.

122
00:12:08,340 --> 00:12:09,700
 So you will see it everywhere.

123
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:11,060
 Not just in MongoDB.

124
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:24,100
 So here is a dictionary from SQL words to MongoDB words.

125
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:27,210
 So a database is still a database.

126
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:27,910
 So a database is still a database.

127
00:12:27,910 --> 00:12:31,520
 A table is called a collection.

128
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:35,930
 A row is a document.

129
00:12:35,930 --> 00:12:38,740
 A column is a field.

130
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:44,370
 The primary key is always called underscore ID.

131
00:12:44,370 --> 00:12:54,570
 Instead of join, we usually put things that belong together inside the same document, which we call embedding.

132
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:04,520
 And instead of SQL, we ask questions with filters, which are just Python dictionaries.

133
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:09,850
 So keep this slide in mind for all week.

134
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:10,930
 I would save this.

135
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:18,940
 Okay, let me say it as a story.

136
00:13:19,570 --> 00:13:26,950
 In SQL, we had a database called Webshop with a table called Customers.

137
00:13:27,370 --> 00:13:30,310
 And each customer was a row with columns.

138
00:13:30,990 --> 00:13:38,560
 In MongoDB, we have a database called Webshop with a collection called Customers.

139
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:41,780
 And each customer is a document with fields.

140
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:45,230
 Same idea, just new words.

141
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:47,860
 The underscore ID is special.

142
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:53,140
 Every document must have one, just like every row needs a primary key.

143
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:58,680
 And the last two rows are where the real difference is.

144
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:04,880
 In SQL, we split data into many tables and join it back together.

145
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:09,600
 In MongoDB, we usually keep things together from the start.

146
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:21,580
 And there is a way to join called Lookup, which we'll look into maybe not tomorrow, maybe the day after it.

147
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:21,940
 I don't know.

148
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:24,200
 We'll see how much time we have.

149
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:26,480
 But we will try to look into that as well.

150
00:14:31,080 --> 00:14:34,990
 Take a photo, if you want, of the slide and keep it open.

151
00:14:35,150 --> 00:14:37,150
 You will see these words all week.

152
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:41,100
 Flexible schema.

153
00:14:41,100 --> 00:14:44,300
 Okay, in SQL, every row had the same columns.

154
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:48,900
 In MongoDB, documents in the same collection can look different.

155
00:14:49,610 --> 00:14:54,690
 Shoes have sizes, but a t-shirt in our shop doesn't, for example.

156
00:14:55,290 --> 00:14:57,240
 That's called a flexible schema.

157
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:00,640
 It's great when your data changes a lot.

158
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:02,720
 But there is a catch.

159
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:06,170
 The database doesn't check anything by default.

160
00:15:06,170 --> 00:15:15,330
 So if you write price with an S instead of with a C, MongoDB happily saves it.

161
00:15:16,070 --> 00:15:19,470
 So with more freedom comes more responsibility.

162
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:25,900
 Maybe throughout the week we will look how to add these rules.

163
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:31,100
 Schema means the plan or structure of the data.

164
00:15:31,570 --> 00:15:33,630
 In SQL, the schema is strict.

165
00:15:33,890 --> 00:15:38,570
 I mean, when we use create table, that's when it's decided.

166
00:15:39,290 --> 00:15:43,590
 In MongoDB, by default, there is no fixed schema.

167
00:15:45,150 --> 00:15:54,640
 If you look at the two documents here, both are products.

168
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:59,980
 Both are in the same collection, but only the sneakers have size.

169
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:04,570
 And MongoDB is fine with that.

170
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:28,120
 So every document has a field called underscore ID.

171
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:32,450
 It works like the primary key.

172
00:16:34,490 --> 00:16:35,530
 It must be unique.

173
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:43,270
 If you don't give one, MongoDB actually creates one for you called an object ID.

174
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:48,510
 It's basically a long code that is unique, even across many computers.

175
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:54,220
 So in our web shop, we choose our own IDs.

176
00:16:55,060 --> 00:16:56,060
 One, two, three.

177
00:16:57,190 --> 00:17:00,990
 So it's easy to compare with the SQL web shop, for example.

178
00:17:03,030 --> 00:17:11,540
 But new things you add without an ID will get an object ID, like you see in front of you here.

179
00:17:12,490 --> 00:17:14,180
 It's going to look something like that.

180
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:20,440
 Underscore ID is always the first field in the document.

181
00:17:21,530 --> 00:17:29,470
 And MongoDB won't let two documents in the same collection have the same one.

182
00:17:29,730 --> 00:17:35,700
 So if you try, you're going to get an error called duplicate key, I think the error is.

183
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:42,570
 I will try to show it to you when we code along.

184
00:17:42,570 --> 00:17:48,600
 But when you don't give it an ID, MongoDB makes an object ID.

185
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:53,910
 It looks like a random jumble of 24 letters and numbers.

186
00:17:54,410 --> 00:18:01,540
 But it's actually built from the time it was created, plus some unique parts.

187
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:07,880
 So two computers can create IDs at the same time without ever colliding.

188
00:18:09,240 --> 00:18:12,150
 And that's very useful for big systems with many servers.

189
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:21,520
 So in our web shop, we choose simple numbers to make it easy to compare with the SQL web shop.

190
00:18:21,620 --> 00:18:23,300
 So customer one is still Anna.

191
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:28,690
 But when you insert a new document without an ID, you will see object's ID appear.

192
00:18:29,370 --> 00:18:34,090
 And that's basically completely normal if that's going to happen with you.

193
00:18:34,550 --> 00:18:35,650
 Okay, data types.

194
00:18:36,830 --> 00:18:41,590
 These are the data types, you see.

195
00:18:42,290 --> 00:18:45,490
 You have strings, numbers, booleans, and dates.

196
00:18:45,810 --> 00:18:46,370
 You know that.

197
00:18:47,250 --> 00:18:48,970
 Array is a list.

198
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:53,280
 And object is a dictionary inside the document.

199
00:18:54,130 --> 00:18:57,000
 Null is nothing, like none in Python.

200
00:18:57,540 --> 00:19:05,530
 The nice thing here is PyMongo translates this automatically.

201
00:19:06,290 --> 00:19:13,690
 So Python list becomes an array, a dictionary becomes an object, and a date time becomes a date.

202
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:26,980
 So let's connect this maybe to Python here, because that's how you will write the data.

203
00:19:27,770 --> 00:19:35,170
 Python string becomes a string, an int becomes an int, a float becomes a double, true and false

204
00:19:35,170 --> 00:19:42,020
 becomes booleans, a Python list becomes an array, and a dictionary becomes an object, and

205
00:19:42,020 --> 00:19:45,540
 also sometimes called embedded documents.

206
00:19:46,490 --> 00:19:53,650
 For dates, we use Python's date time, and MongoDB store it as a real date, actually.

207
00:19:53,830 --> 00:19:58,840
 So we can sort and compare dates properly here.

208
00:20:00,820 --> 00:20:05,900
 And one thing to watch, in JSON, you will see lowercase true and false, and null.

209
00:20:06,580 --> 00:20:13,080
 In Python, it's capital true, as you know, false, true, false, and none is capital.

210
00:20:13,790 --> 00:20:19,330
 So some values, different spellings, I mean, same values, different spellings, basically.

211
00:20:21,130 --> 00:20:23,990
 And if you write true in Python, you're going to get an error, of course.

212
00:20:25,410 --> 00:20:31,550
 That's actually one of the most common small mistakes you will probably encounter this week.

213
00:20:31,830 --> 00:20:34,920
 Maybe, I don't know, just a heads up.

214
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:42,710
 So remember, if you remember the full recipe we built with three joins in SQL, and I don't

215
00:20:42,710 --> 00:20:45,590
 know, that day was pretty confusing, even for me.

216
00:20:46,830 --> 00:20:49,310
 Here is the same order in MongoDB.

217
00:20:49,310 --> 00:20:55,400
 The items are inside the order as a list.

218
00:20:56,500 --> 00:21:07,580
 And each item even has a product name and price copied into it.

219
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:12,880
 To show the receipt, we just read one document.

220
00:21:14,330 --> 00:21:15,490
 No join at all.

221
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:18,750
 And that's the big idea of document databases.

222
00:21:19,530 --> 00:21:23,270
 Data that is read together is stored together as well.

223
00:21:23,270 --> 00:21:40,690
 So if you look at the items list here, each item has the name and the price copied into

224
00:21:40,690 --> 00:21:41,050
 it.

225
00:21:42,950 --> 00:21:43,690
 So why copy them?

226
00:21:44,450 --> 00:21:54,540
 Because if the hoodie costs, for example, 649 next month, this old receipt must still say

227
00:21:54,540 --> 00:21:55,420
 599.

228
00:21:56,990 --> 00:21:59,710
 The price Anna actually paid.

229
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:06,180
 We did the same thing in SQL with unit price.

230
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:15,850
 So to show this receipt in SQL, we needed orders, order items, some products, and basically

231
00:22:15,850 --> 00:22:16,690
 three joins.

232
00:22:18,430 --> 00:22:21,550
 But here we only...

233
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:24,440
 This gives us everything.

234
00:22:31,910 --> 00:22:34,940
 Okay, but if I ask you one question here to the class.

235
00:22:35,430 --> 00:22:36,400
 What is...

236
00:22:36,400 --> 00:22:40,060
 What's still not inside this document?

237
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:43,260
 What do we don't see here?

238
00:22:50,110 --> 00:22:58,170
 Well, the customer's name.

239
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:01,350
 There is only customer ID, as you can see here.

240
00:23:03,190 --> 00:23:04,710
 And that's on purpose.

241
00:23:05,340 --> 00:23:11,540
 Hopefully, by tomorrow or after tomorrow, maybe, we'll talk about when to put things inside

242
00:23:11,540 --> 00:23:14,440
 a document and when to just point to them.

